Root-cause walkthrough
----------------------

`20230706_124619.jpg` is a Samsung Galaxy S23 Ultra camera JPEG,
4000x3000, 4063102 bytes, baseline (SOF0), 3-component YCbCr, with
DRI restart markers.

Three diff bytes pre-fix, all immediately before RST markers:

  offset 1129739: src=0x00 ours=0x7F (before FFD0 / RST0)
  offset 2431077: src=0x00 ours=0x3F (before FFD1 / RST1)
  offset 3344390: src=0x00 ours=0x7F (before FFD2 / RST2)

Mechanism: the entropy stream from the source camera encoder ends
mid-byte at each RST boundary, with the partial byte padded by ZERO
bits (non-spec-compliant but legal). The spec-recommended emitter
(what djxl uses by default) pads with ONE bits, producing the
shifted-form bytes our reconstruction emitted. The shifted-form
roundtrip is BIT-EXACT for entropy correctness (decodes to identical
pixels) but byte-INEXACT.

The fix captures the source's actual pad bits at each entropy-segment
terminator and replays them via the existing JBRD writer path. djxl's
`JumpToByteBoundary` consumes the explicit bits when
`jpg.has_zero_padding_bit == true`, producing the source's exact byte
values.

DO NOT
------

  - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement (per W44-66).
    The bytes that move are JBRD entropy-pad-bit bytes, structurally
    deterministic, not FP-numeric.
  - DO NOT default-flip the tracking in `decode_coefficients()`
    (zenjpeg's legacy entry point). Its zero-overhead contract is
    load-bearing for tight-loop callers; only the new
    `decode_coefficients_with_jbrd_metadata()` carries the tracking
    cost.
  - DO NOT mark `has_zero_padding_bit = true` unconditionally; libjxl
    only sets it when at least one bit is actually 0, and the
    optimisation matters (5-7 bits saved per JBRD header). The
    `padding_bits` Vec must be DROPPED when the flag is false — see
    `decode_coefficients_with_jbrd_metadata` in `decode/mod.rs` for
    the canonical pattern.

